## Objective

Develop the existing SAT/H(s)H geometric solvers — Universal Indicatrix, Whirligig, Spheres, and Gendarme Graticule — into configurable computational tools that can:

- accept clearly defined inputs;
- apply explicit geometric and/or physical couplings;
- calculate forced geometric relationships;
- compare alternative representations;
- expose invariants, residuals, symmetries, asymmetries, and transitions;
- support multiple physical interpretations without confusing those interpretations with the solver primitives;
- be implemented as reusable scripts rather than one-off diagrams.

The aim is not merely to document what the solvers historically were, but to determine what each can become.

